  "title": "Social Rejection Hurts Physically",
  "emoji": "❤️",
  "color": "#9333EA",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply Social Rejection Hurts Physically.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Being excluded from a simple ball-tossing game activates the same brain regions as a broken arm.",
      "visual": "⚾"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "The Cyberball experiment showed that even virtual exclusion by strangers triggers real neural pain responses.",
      "visual": "💻"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The dorsal anterior cingulate cortex and insula light up during both physical pain and social rejection.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Taking acetaminophen (Tylenol) reduced the emotional pain of rejection in controlled studies.",
      "visual": "💊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "This overlap exists because social bonds were essential for survival—exclusion once meant death.",
      "visual": "☠️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Heartbreak isn't a metaphor. Your brain processes it as genuine pain.",
      "visual": "💔"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is the Cyberball experiment?",
    "options": [
      "A virtual ball-tossing game used to study social exclusion",
      "A test of physical reflexes",
      "A memory assessment tool"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
